WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
I asked my class which fruit they

2
00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:05.270
like most, and drew a chart.

3
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.550
Mangoes won by a mile!

4
00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:12.690
Then Musa said: Miss, your

5
00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.470
bars don't start at zero.

6
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.020
Today: draw charts from your data,

7
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.240
and check that they're fair.

8
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.430
A pictogram uses little pictures.

9
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:24.990
Each picture stands for some

10
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.510
votes: one picture, two pupils.

11
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.500
Always write what one picture means.

12
00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.350
A bar chart uses bars.

13
00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.410
The taller the bar, the more votes.

14
00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.970
Every bar has the same width, a label

15
00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:42.070
underneath, and starts at zero.

16
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.440
To read a bar chart, find the

17
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.120
tallest bar and the shortest.

18
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.710
Then compare: oranges got five

19
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:51.910
more votes than bananas.

20
00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:53.710
Quick check!

21
00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.350
In a pictogram, one orange

22
00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.010
picture means two pupils.

23
00:00:58.820 --> 00:00:59.960
There are four pictures.

24
00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.070
How many pupils is that?

25
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:03.640
Eight.

26
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.660
Four pictures, two pupils each:

27
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.740
two, four, six, eight.

28
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.530
Charts must be fair.

29
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.770
Bars start at zero, or a small

30
00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:16.990
difference looks huge.

31
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.660
And every answer gets its

32
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.260
bar, even the small ones.

33
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.210
Look for odd values too.

34
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.320
If a soil reading jumps to zero and

35
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.220
straight back, something went wrong.

36
00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:31.450
On a chart, it jumps out.

37
00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.590
In the app, a week of soil

38
00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.010
readings from the school garden.

39
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.720
Find the red dots: the

40
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.320
readings that can't be right.

41
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.200
Tap “Leave them out”, and see

42
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.420
the lowest reading change.

43
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.500
Your turn.

44
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.260
Which of these charts is fair?

45
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.180
The one with bars from zero,

46
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:54.760
and every answer shown.

47
00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.380
Remember: a pictogram says

48
00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:58.980
what one picture means.

49
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:01.540
The tallest bar has the most.

50
00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.450
And a fair chart starts at zero.

51
00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.440
Try it tomorrow: turn your tally from

52
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.800
the first lesson into a bar chart.

53
00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.490
Give it a title and labels.

54
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.240
And start the bars at zero!
